WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:11.520
When you know enough about your data, you&nbsp;
can help your machine connect the dots with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.640
supervised learning. In supervised learning,&nbsp;
you show the machine the connection between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:22.640
different variables and known outcomes. In machine&nbsp;
learning, this is called the supervised learning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.840
It's called supervised learning because it's&nbsp;
already tagged with identifying information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:33.280
Imagine you wanted to train a machine to help you&nbsp;
predict how long it will take you to drive home.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:39.680
You would start by creating a set of labeled&nbsp;
data. This data would include weather conditions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:45.520
time of day, and whether it's a holiday.&nbsp;
These are your inputs. The output would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:51.280
the amount of time it took to drive home on that&nbsp;
particular day. In this case, your independent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:56.880
variables would be the input and your dependent&nbsp;
variable would be the output. Here you want to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:01.600
different machine learning algorithms to map the&nbsp;
relationships between these different variables.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:07.040
You can use statistical regression for this&nbsp;
and determine how the independent variables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:12.560
affect the dependent variable. You intuitively&nbsp;
know that if it's raining outside, it will take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:18.320
you longer to drive home, but machines have to&nbsp;
rely on data and statistics. They have to look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.920
at the length of your commute and compare&nbsp;
it to labeled data it has on the weather.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:26.960
Let's see how we can create a supervised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:29.920
machine learning model to help&nbsp;
us determine our commute time.&nbsp;

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.520
The first thing you want to&nbsp;
do is create a training set.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:41.120
Based on this training set, your machine might see&nbsp;
there's a direct relationship between the amount&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:46.560
of rain and the time it takes to get home. The&nbsp;
more it rains, the longer you'll be on the road.&nbsp;

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.680
It might also see there's a connection between&nbsp;
the time you leave work and the time you'll be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:58.720
on the road. The closer you are to 5 p.m.,&nbsp;
the longer it takes for you to get home.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:04.640
Your machine finds some of the relationships&nbsp;
with your labeled data. This is the start of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:10.640
your data model. It begins to understand concepts&nbsp;
like how rain impacts the way people drive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:15.680
It also starts to see that there are more&nbsp;
people who travel during certain times of day.&nbsp;

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.880
Then your machine takes this training set&nbsp;
and applies it to a test set of your data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.240
It looks to see if the model in this training set&nbsp;
holds true when you're looking at many more days.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:33.440
You could ask the computer to predict how&nbsp;
long it will take you to drive home each day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:36.480
and then give it feedback on how&nbsp;
accurate it was in its prediction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:43.040
Over time, the machine will learn and adapt its&nbsp;
model to improve the output. Your machine will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:49.840
continue to tune the model in the training set and&nbsp;
you can make adjustments based on the new data.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:55.040
This is pretty similar to how humans&nbsp;
learn. Think about when you learned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:59.680
how to drive. You started in a small&nbsp;
parking lot with an adult guiding you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:04.960
Then you got to the point where you could drive&nbsp;
predictably and safely in this smaller space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:09.680
Then once you built up enough confidence,&nbsp;
you'd head out into the open road.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.800
You won't learn everything you need to know about&nbsp;
driving in the parking lot but you should get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:20.000
enough data to help you get started. Chances&nbsp;
are you'll still learn a lot of new skills,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.240
and hopefully you'll continue to improve&nbsp;
over time until you become an expert driver.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.760
The key thing to remember is that in supervised&nbsp;
machine learning you know a lot more about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:36.960
training data. You can feed labeled data&nbsp;
into the machine that's easily classified.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:43.760
So, with your commute home you know more about the&nbsp;
weather, holidays, and time of day. This labeled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:54.560
data is the key difference between supervised&nbsp;
learning and other forms of machine learning.

